{"name":"Crushed Stone","hex":"#bcaa9f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crushed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of crush"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pulverized, rendered into small, disconnected fragments.","Broken, saddened, depressed, devastated."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crushed"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crushed stone","blocks":[{"text":"Crushed stone or angular rock is a form of construction aggregate, typically produced by mining a suitable rock deposit and breaking the removed rock down to the desired size using crushers. It is distinct from naturally occurring gravel, which is produced by natural processes of weathering and erosion and typically has a more rounded shape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/20mm-aggregate.jpg/330px-20mm-aggregate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crushed_stone"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Hanson_Aggregates_Bellefonte_PA.jpg/500px-Hanson_Aggregates_Bellefonte_PA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Hanson_Aggregates_Bellefonte_PA.jpg/1280px-Hanson_Aggregates_Bellefonte_PA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Hanson_Aggregates_Bellefonte_PA.jpg/500px-Hanson_Aggregates_Bellefonte_PA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Crushed limestone quarry near Bellefonte, Pennsylvania","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHanson_Aggregates_Bellefonte_PA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/CrushedStoneBarge.jpg/500px-CrushedStoneBarge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/CrushedStoneBarge.jpg/500px-CrushedStoneBarge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A crushed stone barge in China","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrushedStoneBarge.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/2d_Av_subway_crush_stone_72_jeh.jpg/500px-2d_Av_subway_crush_stone_72_jeh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/2d_Av_subway_crush_stone_72_jeh.jpg/1280px-2d_Av_subway_crush_stone_72_jeh.jpg?utm_sou